[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Observability, Logging, and Distributed Tracing in REST APIs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As modern software architectures shift from monolithic applications to distributed microservices, debugging production issues in REST APIs becomes increasingly complex. A single user request may traverse multiple independent services, making it difficult to trace failures, latency bottlenecks, and unexpected behavior. This tutorial covers the three pillars of observability: Metrics, Logging, and Distributed Tracing, focusing on Correlation IDs for end-to-end request tracking."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding the Pillars of Observability"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Metrics: Aggregated numerical data about your system, such as CPU utilization, request rate (RPM), error rates, and response time percentiles (p95, p99)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Logging: Discrete, timestamped records of events that occurred within the application. Essential for understanding the internal state during a specific execution flow."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Distributed Tracing: The mechanism of tracking a request as it propagates across multiple services and network boundaries, mapping the entire dependency tree."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Correlation IDs in REST APIs"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Correlation ID (often passed as the "X-Correlation-ID" or "X-Request-ID" HTTP header) is a unique identifier assigned to an incoming HTTP request. This ID is passed along to every downstream service and included in all log entries, tying disparate log lines to a single user interaction."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const express = require('express');
const { v4: uuidv4 } = require('uuid');
const app = express();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const correlationId = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || uuidv4();
  req.correlationId = correlationId;
  res.setHeader('X-Correlation-ID', correlationId);
  next();
});

app.get('/api/resource', (req, res) => {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    correlationId: req.correlationId,
    message: 'Processing resource request'
  }));
  res.status(200).json({ status: 'success' });
});

app.listen(3000);"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Structured Logging Best Practices"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Plain text logs are difficult to query and parse. Structured logging (typically outputting JSON) ensures that log aggregators like ELK, Datadog, or Grafana Loki can index fields such as correlationId, timestamp, log level, and endpoint."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"{
  "timestamp": "2023-10-25T14:32:01.123Z",
  "level": "INFO",
  "correlationId": "a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef0123456789",
  "service": "payment-service",
  "endpoint": "/api/v1/payments",
  "message": "Payment processed successfully"
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Integrating Distributed Tracing with OpenTelemetry"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"While correlation IDs link logs, distributed tracing standards like OpenTelemetry provide deep visibility into the exact execution duration of HTTP calls, database queries, and message queue publishes within a trace context comprising traces and spans."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { trace } = require('@opentelemetry/api');

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
provider.register();

const tracer = trace.getTracer('user-service');

async function handleRequest(req, res) {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('handleRequest');
  // Business logic here
  span.end();
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By enforcing correlation IDs at the API gateway, adopting structured JSON logging, and instrumenting your REST APIs with OpenTelemetry, you achieve true production observability. This drastically reduces Mean Time to Detection (MTTD) and Mean Time to Resolution (MTTR) for incidents in distributed systems."}]
